# Cookies

A **cookie** is a small piece of data stored in the browser by a website.

Cookies help websites:

* Remember logins
* Store preferences
* Track sessions
* Personalize content

---

## Cookie Structure

```http
Set-Cookie: sessionid=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure
```

### Parts

| Part      | Meaning              |
| --------- | -------------------- |
| sessionid | Cookie name          |
| abc123    | Cookie value         |
| HttpOnly  | JS cannot access it  |
| Secure    | Sent only over HTTPS |

---

## Types of Cookies

| Type              | Purpose                      |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Session Cookie    | Deleted after browser closes |
| Persistent Cookie | Stored for longer time       |
| Secure Cookie     | Sent only over HTTPS         |
| HttpOnly Cookie   | Prevents JavaScript access   |
| SameSite Cookie   | Helps stop CSRF attacks      |

---

## Session vs Cookies

| Feature      | Session              | Cookie                   |
| ------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Stored Where | Server               | Browser                  |
| Size         | Large possible       | Small                    |
| Security     | More secure          | Less secure              |
| Lifetime     | Controlled by server | Controlled by browser    |
| Used For     | Authentication state | Session ID & preferences |

---

## How Sessions and Cookies Work Together

### Step-by-step

1. User logs in
2. Server creates session:

   ```text
   Session ID = XYZ123
   ```
3. Server sends cookie:

   ```http
   Set-Cookie: PHPSESSID=XYZ123
   ```
4. Browser stores cookie
5. Browser sends cookie on every request:

   ```http
   Cookie: PHPSESSID=XYZ123
   ```
6. Server verifies session ID

---

## Important Security Risks

| Attack            | Description                                       |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Session Hijacking | Stealing session cookie                           |
| Session Fixation  | Forcing victim to use known session ID            |
| Cookie Theft      | XSS steals cookies                                |
| CSRF              | Browser sends authenticated request automatically |

---

## Protection Methods

| Protection       | Purpose                        |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------ |
| HTTPS            | Encrypt cookies                |
| HttpOnly         | Stops JS cookie theft          |
| Secure Flag      | HTTPS only                     |
| SameSite         | Reduces CSRF                   |
| Session Rotation | Changes session ID after login |
| Short Timeout    | Limits attacker access         |

---

## Example HTTP Headers

### Server Response

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Set-Cookie: sessionid=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict
```

### Browser Request

```http
GET /dashboard HTTP/1.1
Cookie: sessionid=abc123
```

---

## Real-Life Analogy

| Concept         | Real-life Example               |
| --------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Session         | Temporary access pass in office |
| Cookie          | Card storing your access number |
| Session Timeout | Pass expires after inactivity   |

---

## Common Cookie Flags

| Flag            | Meaning                 |
| --------------- | ----------------------- |
| Secure          | HTTPS only              |
| HttpOnly        | No JavaScript access    |
| SameSite=Lax    | Partial CSRF protection |
| SameSite=Strict | Strong CSRF protection  |
| Expires         | Expiry date             |
| Max-Age         | Lifetime in seconds     |

